  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...While wind and seas have come down, minor \nsoundside flooding (1 ft or less) is still possible for mainly \nDowneast Carteret county through the next several high tide \ncycles. The high astronomical tides peak today, and already high\nwater levels due to last week's wind event will keep water \nlevels elevated mainly around the high tide cycles through the \nnext couple days. Standing water on roadways, and in low lying \nor poor drainage areas, could have minor impacts for travelers \nin especially vulnerable areas prone to the tidal flooding. \nElevated water levels will persist through Wednesday, but levels\nare not expected to be as high as they were the last several \ndays. A Coastal Flood Advisory is in effect for the areas most \nat risk through today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An upper level ridge will expand east across \nthe Southeastern U.S. through the week. Beneath the ridge, light\nsoutherly flow and increasing low-level thicknesses should \nsupport a period of above to well above normal temperatures, \nespecially on Thursday and Friday. Forecast aids are now showing\nan increasing signal for above normal temperatures, especially \non Friday, adding confidence to the potential. For reference, \nnormal highs in early October are around 80 degrees, and \nforecast highs are in the mid to upper 80s for much of ENC, with\nsome 90s possible inland. Persistent southerly flow and \nincreasing low-level moisture should help to keep overnight lows\nhigher as well, and well above the normal lows of around 60.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Over the weekend, a weak area of low pressure \nis forecast to lift NE along a cold front that will be advancing\nsoutheast through the Eastern U.S. Ahead of the front and low, \na pool of moisture over the Gulf is forecast to get pulled north\ninto the Carolinas, supporting an increased risk of showers. \nThere may be just enough instability present to support a risk \nof thunderstorms as well, especially on Sunday. For now, \nensemble guidance is showing about a 30-50% chance of exceeding \n1\" of rain with this system. Any rain will be helpful, \nespecially for the coastal plain of ENC that is currently in \nmoderate to extreme drought.",
